/-!
# The canonical and boundary indices of the cyclic quotient model

Let `K` be an algebraically closed field of characteristic zero, `m ≥ 1`, `gcd(q, m) = 1`, and
let `T = T_{m,q} = Spec K[[x, y]]^{μ_m}` be the cyclic quotient model
(`MMP.Flips.cyclicQuotientModel`), with boundaries `C^{(1)} = C^{(1)}_{m,q}`, the reduced image
of `(x = 0)`, and `C^{(2)} = C^{(2)}_{m,q}`, the reduced image of `(xy = 0)`.

Then `T` is normal, its class group is cyclic of order `m`, generated by `[C^{(1)}]`, and `T` is
regular exactly when `m = 1`. Both `C^{(1)}` and `K_T + C^{(1)}` have Cartier index exactly `m`.
The divisor `K_T + C^{(2)}` is Cartier: `ω_T(C^{(2)})` is generated by `dx ∧ dy / (xy)`, and the
reflexive power `ω_T(C^{(1)})^{[m]}` is generated by `(dx ∧ dy / x)^{⊗ m}`.

Write `A = K[[x, y]]`, `R = A^{μ_m}`, `C = C^{(1)}` and `C'` for the image of `(y = 0)`, so
that `C^{(2)} = C + C'`. Every height-one prime of `R` is cut out by a semi-invariant of `A`,
which gives `div(x^m) = m C`, `div(y^m) = m C'`, and shows that `Cl(T)` is cyclic of order
`m`, generated by `[C]`; the class of `C'` has order `m` as well, because `q` is prime to `m`.
On the local scheme `T` a divisor is Cartier exactly when it is principal. The element `x^m` is
irreducible in `R`; for `m > 1` it divides `(x^i y)^m = (x^m)^i y^m`, with `0 < i < m` and
`i + q ≡ 0 (mod m)`, but not `x^i y`, so `R` is not factorial and hence not regular.

`R` is a free module over the regular ring `P = K[[u, v]]`, `u ↦ x^m`, `v ↦ y^m`, on the
monomials `x^i y^j`, `0 ≤ i, j < m`, of weight `i + q j ≡ 0`. The residue pairing
`⟨g, r⟩ = res(g r dx ∧ dy)`, the coefficient series of `x^{m-1} y^{m-1}` in `g r`, identifies the
module of invariant forms `g dx ∧ dy` with `Hom_P(R, P) = RHom_P(R, P)`, which is a dualizing
module of `R` because `P` is regular. Multiplication by `x y` identifies the invariant forms with
the global sections of `𝒪_T(-C^{(2)})`, so `-C^{(2)}` is a canonical divisor. A dualizing module
of the local ring `R` is unique up to isomorphism, so every canonical divisor `K_T` has
`𝒪_T(K_T) ≅ 𝒪_T(-C^{(2)})`, that is `K_T ∼ -C^{(2)}`, which gives the Cartier statements.

## Main results

* `MMP.Flips.cyclicQuotientModel_invariants`: the statement above.

## Implementation notes

* The canonical module is realized by `μ_m`-invariant `2`-forms: the `R`-module of `f ∈ K[[x, y]]`
  with `f dx ∧ dy` invariant, that is `ζ^{1+q} f(ζx, ζ^q y) = f` (the determinant character of the
  action), is asserted to be a dualizing `R`-module, `R = K[[x, y]]^{μ_m}`. A canonical divisor
  of `T` is a Weil divisor `K_T` whose divisorial sheaf `𝒪_T(K_T)` is a dualizing module.
* The form `dx ∧ dy / (xy)` is invariant with divisor `-C^{(2)}` on `T`; its generating
  `ω_T(C^{(2)})` is the statement that `-C^{(2)}` is a canonical divisor. As
  `(dx ∧ dy / x)^{⊗ m} = y^m (dx ∧ dy / (xy))^{⊗ m}`, its generating `ω_T(C^{(1)})^{[m]}` is the
  equality `div(y^m) = m (C^{(2)} - C^{(1)})` of Weil divisors on `T`, with `y^m ∈ R`.
* "Index exactly `m`" is stated as: `n D` is Cartier if and only if `m ∣ n`. The class group
  statement is: every divisor is linearly equivalent to a multiple `n C^{(1)}`, and `n C^{(1)}`
  is principal exactly when `m ∣ n`.
* The canonical module is computed directly over the regular subring `K[[x^m, y^m]]` of `R`,
  over which `R` is finite and free, rather than on polynomial local models transported along
  completion: the dualizing complex of `R` is then `RHom_P(R, P)` by Stacks Tag 0AX0, and freeness
  makes it a module in degree zero. The scheme-level dualizing module is obtained from the
  ring-level one on the single affine chart `T` (Stacks Tag 0A86), and the comparison with an
  arbitrary canonical divisor goes through the stalk at the closed point, which is the module of
  global sections because `R` is local.

## References

* [J. Kollár et al., *Flips and abundance for algebraic threefolds*][KollarFlips], 16.6
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0AX0](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0AX0)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0A86](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0A86)
-/

